Z||000000||KRMFiero||08-25-2001||05:38 PM||||The fire Dept I am on resently got a Motorola Base Station that was originaly in our towns Police station: <BR>Model: C73RTB-1144EM-SP320 <BR>Serial: 301CDC0133 <P>It currently has our deptarments fire freq. in for F2, F1 is the police - can we change that to something else? - how?<P>Also the Mic seems very weak - does anyone have any diagrams to see how this modle should be hooked up? <P>If anyone could give us any links to any type of owners manuels or anythign it would be very usefully <P>Thanks<BR>Kyle (WLFD)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||08-25-2001||06:41 PM||||Hey Kyle!<BR>The best answer I can give, is to call a Motorola shop...<BR>Seems to me, they have the best chance of having the manual and parts, or can order them for you...<BR>If they are reluctant, try an amature radio forum...<BR>HMMMM... Does Motorola have a web site???<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||08-26-2001||02:17 PM||||Kyle,<P>Worked for Motorola for 10 years. Used to work on those. <BR>Fierowrecker is pretty much right. <BR>The frequency is determined by plug-in "channel elements." They're actually a crystal and a tuned network, all in one housing.<BR>Anybody can replace them, if you can get the frequency you want. <BR>The difficulty lies in getting the station tuned on frequency, to FCC specs. At the minimum, it will require a frequency counter capable of receiving signals in the 150-170 MHz range. There's a "tweak" on the bottom of the channel element that must be adjusted.<BR>Tuning the receiver will require a signal generator that will also work in the same band. <BR>If you go to the Motorola shop (Call several. Some are waayyy more accommodating than others), they may even have channel elements in the frequency you want, if it's a common frequency. We used to keep drawers full of 'em. If your old channel elements are good, they may even trade with you, and just charge you for the labor of replacing them and tweaking them to specs.<BR>There is also a company by the name of International Crystal, that will rebuild your original channel elements to whatever frequency you need. Not sure about cost, but if memory serves, it wasn't too terrible. <BR>Still need the test equipment to set it up correctly, though.<P>Having said all that...<BR>The FCC used to require that radio transmitters be verified for operation within FCC specs, every year. They also required that anything done to a transmitter be done by a licensed tech. This may have changed, but I'm not sure. <BR>Who did the work on the equipment when it was the PD's? May be the best bet to get the work done.<P>Hope this helps some.<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-26-2001).]||||reg||1
